# fstab — real-world examples

## See what is currently mounted

```sh
cat /etc/mtab      # or: findmnt
```

## Mount by filesystem LABEL

```sh
sudo blkid                       # display existing UUID/LABEL partition properties
sudo e2label /dev/sdc1 /data     # set the LABEL to /data
sudo vim /etc/fstab
```

```text
LABEL=/data  /data  ext4  defaults  1 2
```

## Mount by UUID (preferred — survives device renames)

```sh
sudo blkid /dev/sdd1             # copy the UUID
```

```text
UUID=<uuid>  /srv/media  xfs  defaults  0 0
```

## Apply fstab changes without rebooting

```sh
sudo mount -a                    # mount everything listed in fstab
sudo systemctl daemon-reload     # systemd generates mount units from fstab
```

## NFS share from the media server, don't hang boot if it's down

```text
mediaserver:/srv/media  /mnt/media  nfs  defaults,_netdev,nofail  0 0
```

## Test an entry before trusting it at boot

```sh
sudo umount /srv/media && sudo mount /srv/media   # remount just that entry
findmnt --verify                                  # sanity-check fstab syntax
```

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | device (UUID=, LABEL=, /dev/..., host:/path) |
| 2 | mount point |
| 3 | filesystem type (ext4, xfs, nfs, ...) |
| 4 | options (defaults, noatime, nofail, _netdev) |
| 5 | dump flag (0 = skip) |
| 6 | fsck order (0 = skip, 1 = root, 2 = others) |
